[{:seon.task/name "a-long-string-does-not-starve-its-siblings"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: an eval item {:form \"(code-of 0)\" :out <9,600 chars of pprint output> :value (vec (range 2000))} printed under 545 tokens (`seon.print/print-value`, the last-evals share) shows 1,584 characters of :out and :value as `[…2000 (get-in (nth (my.turn/evals) 0) [:value])]`: not one element of the value the agent asked for. Cause: `seon.print.select` ranks a long string before a collection (`member-rank`) and then allots the string's characters like one member, greedily, while the collection's members are a level deeper, so breadth-first never reaches them. Correct: a long string's characters are allotted in the same rounds as its siblings' members (a string is a sequence of lines for allotment), so every sibling shows its first part before any one deepens. Every value with a long string beside a collection benefits (contract errors, test expected/actual, the context). Replaces nothing; the law tests in seon.print-test stay green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-test/a-long-string-shares-the-budget-with-its-siblings}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-long-name-is-cut-like-any-text"
  :seon.task/plan "Since the one string cutter (`seon.print.select/break-at`, `seon.print.emit/cut-words`), a string in a value and prose both cut as `\"prefix\"…+7279 chars in 88 more lines (subs result/h3 19523)`, but a keyword or symbol longer than :seon.config.render.agent/max-name still prints a bare `prefix…+N` with no unit and no form (`seon.print.select/tree-node`'s :name row; `seon.print.select/node-continuation` names no form for a name). Blind scoring on the printer-eval corpus ranked the bare `…+N` last most often (notes/printer-eval/README.md: no unit). Correct: a name's cut says `…+N chars` and names the form that reads the whole name (`(str (get-in result/h3 path))`, or `(name …)`), through the same cut words; the print-test laws then require a form on every cut through a handle with no exception for names. Replaces the name row's own cut text."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-test/the-text-stays-inside-its-budget}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-first-member-that-fits-is-shown"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by seon.print-test/the-text-stays-inside-its-budget on jolt/writeback-order (2026-10-23; the branch moved the test's digest, so its seed is 297520838378234955): ```seon.print/print-value``` of `[[[[s]]]]`, s an 82-character string of a, quote and newline characters (the test's shrunk value), at {:budget 60 :handle 'result/e0123456789ab} answers the insufficient-floor-budget error, \"budget of 192 characters cannot show this value's first part, which needs 74\". It is ```seon.print.select/select```'s first-member branch (select.clj ~650): ```seon.print.select/allot``` showed the root collection with no member though root and its first member's least form (74) fit the budget (192), so the cause is in allot's placing of the first member, not in the check. Correct: allot shows the first member whenever its least form fits, and the error's needed exceeds its budget whenever it is answered. Proof: the test at that seed, and the value written out as an explicit assertion in it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-test/the-text-stays-inside-its-budget}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-first-entry-that-fits-is-shown"
  :seon.task/plan "Left by a-first-member-that-fits-is-shown (2026-10-23): ```seon.print.select/fill-map``` reserves the cut's room before its first entry, so a map whose first entry's least form fits the budget only without that reserve answers the insufficient-floor-budget error, where a vector (```seon.print.select/first-at-least```) shows its first member. Correct: a map's first entry is held to the same floor as a collection's first member. Proof: a map at a budget just above its first entry's least form prints within it, its cut naming the form."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-test/the-text-stays-inside-its-budget}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-whole-value-that-fits-is-never-refused-for-its-cut-text"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction (found 2026-10-23 by the vocabulary rename's prove; the property's seed is a digest of the test, so any edit to print_test.clj reseeds it): `seon.print-test/the-text-stays-inside-its-budget` with {:seed 406661181079190491} shrinks to the raw value [[[s]]] (s a 98-character string of quotes and one newline) printed at budget 60 under the handle result/e0123456789ab: `print-value` answers the insufficient-floor-budget error map, which has no :text, and `lawful?` throws on `(cuts-of nil)`. The value is 52 tokens whole and fits; with the short handle result/h it prints. Cause: `fill-members` (src/seon/print/select.clj, the last-member fallback) sizes the room against `(sized context (assoc node :kids kids :pending nil))`, a skeleton that keeps :total 1 with 0 kids, so `more?` is true and `flat-width` charges the cut text `…1 (drop 0 result/e0123456789ab)` that does not exist once the last member shows whole; the member is refused, n stays 0, and `select` returns the error. Correct: size the skeleton as the finished collection (`:total (count kids)`), so no cut is charged; check `fill-map` for the same shape. Proof: red then green on that input; and `lawful?` accepts the error only when the whole value cannot fit its floor. Replaces nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-test/the-text-stays-inside-its-budget}}]

